@page "/components/HxScrollspy"

<ApiDoc Type="typeof(HxScrollspy)">

    <DocHeading Title="How it works" />
    <p>
        Scrollspy needs two components to work together. The <code>HxScrollspy</code> component wraps
        the area to spy on, and the <a href="/components/@nameof(HxNav)"><code>@nameof(HxNav)</code></a> or a list-group (with <code><a href="/components/@nameof(HxScrollspyNavLink)">@nameof(HxScrollspyNavLink)</a>s</code>) represent the navigation.
        These components need to be associated using the <code>HxScrollspy.TargetId</code> parameter.
        For more details, see the <a href="https://getbootstrap.com/docs/6.0/components/scrollspy/">Bootstrap documentation</a>.
    </p>

    <p>The scrollspy navigation has to use a custom <code>HxScrollspyNavLink</code> to work around the <code>&lt;base&gt;</code> Blazor requirement and the inability of Bootstrap to interpret the <code>page#fragment</code> form of link.
        When clicked, <code>HxScrollspyNavLink</code> scrolls only the scrollable container of the target section (the rest of the page stays where it is),
        updates the URL fragment without adding a browser history entry and moves the focus to the section.</p>

    <p>For anchor-fragment navigation (<code>&lt;a href="#id"&gt;</code>) to work in <code>net7.0</code> and earlier, the page has to host the <a href="/components/HxAnchorFragmentNavigation">HxAnchorFragmentNavigation</a> component.</p>

    <DocAlert Type="DocAlertType.Warning">
        The current component design expects the scrollspy to be used in a <b>dedicated scrollable container</b>
        (such as <code>&lt;div&gt;</code> or <a href="/components/HxDrawer">HxDrawer</a>).<br />
        For full-page scrollspy, the spying has to be attached directly to <code>&lt;body&gt;</code> (will be implemented later as needed?).
    </DocAlert>

    <p>
        The active section is the deepest one whose top has scrolled past the <i>activation line</i> near the top of the container (<code>12%</code> by default).
        At the very bottom of the container, the last section gets activated even if its top never reaches the line.
        Use the <code>TopMargin</code> parameter to move the activation line (e.g. <code>96px</code> to sit below a sticky header),
        or take full control with the raw <code>IntersectionObserver</code> <code>RootMargin</code> (and <code>Threshold</code>) parameters.
    </p>

    <DocHeading Title="Navbar" />
    <p>
        Scroll the area below the <a href="/components/@nameof(HxNavbar)"><code>@nameof(HxNavbar)</code></a> and watch the active class change.
        Open the menu and watch the menu items being highlighted as well (the menu toggle gets the <code>.active</code> class too).
        The demo also sets <code>RootMargin="0px 0px -40%"</code> and enables <code>SmoothScroll</code>.
    </p>
    <Demo Type="typeof(HxScrollspy_Demo_Navbar)" />

    <DocHeading Title="Static content with HxNav navigation" />
    <Demo Type="typeof(HxScrollspy_Demo_HxNav)" />

    <DocHeading Title="Static content with list-group navigation" />
    <Demo Type="typeof(HxScrollspy_Demo_ListGroup)" />

    <DocHeading Title="Nested navigation" />
    <p>
        Scrollspy also works with nested <a href="/components/@nameof(HxNav)"><code>@nameof(HxNav)</code></a>s.
        When a nested link becomes active, the link right before the nested <code>HxNav</code> (its parent) gets the <code>.active</code> class too.
    </p>
    <Demo Type="typeof(HxScrollspy_Demo_Nested)" />

    <DocHeading Title="Simple anchors" />
    <p>
        Scrollspy is not limited to navs and list groups, it works with any <code>&lt;a&gt;</code> elements inside the target element.
        Plain anchors do not have any <code>.active</code> styling, so you have to provide your own CSS.
    </p>
    <DocAlert Type="DocAlertType.Warning">
        Blazor resolves plain <code>&lt;a href="#id"&gt;</code> links against <code>&lt;base href&gt;</code>, so a click would navigate away from the current page.
        Either enable <code>SmoothScroll</code> (<code>HxScrollspy</code> then handles the click and scrolls to the section itself), as in the demo below,
        or use <code>@nameof(HxScrollspyNavLink)</code>.
    </DocAlert>
    <Demo Type="typeof(HxScrollspy_Demo_SimpleAnchors)" />

    <DocHeading Title="Non-visible elements" />
    <p>
        Target elements that are not visible are ignored and their navigation links won't receive the <code>.active</code> class.
        A scrollspy initialized in a non-visible wrapper (e.g. an inactive tab of <a href="/components/@nameof(HxTabPanel)"><code>@nameof(HxTabPanel)</code></a>
        or a collapsed <a href="/components/@nameof(HxCollapse)"><code>@nameof(HxCollapse)</code></a>) ignores all its target elements.
        Call <code>HxScrollspy.RefreshAsync()</code> once the wrapper has been rendered visible to pick up the targets
        (e.g. from <code>HxCollapse.OnShown</code>).
    </p>

    <DocHeading Title="Dynamic content (async loaded data)" />
    <DocAlert Type="DocAlertType.Warning">
        <p>
            Bootstrap scrollspy collects the sections to observe (the elements targeted by the navigation links) when activated (i.e. <code>OnAfterRenderAsync</code> within first render).
            If the content changes dynamically, you have to refresh the scrollspy manually by calling <code>HxScrollspy.RefreshAsync()</code>.
        </p>
        <p>
            A similar issue applies for fragment-navigation. You have to postpone the initial fragment navigation to happen after the data gets loaded and the UI renders the final version
            (by setting <code>&lt;HxAnchorFragmentNavigation Automation="HxAnchorFragmentNavigation.SamePage"&gt;</code>
            and calling <code>HxAnchorFragmentNavigation.ScrollToCurrentUriFragmentAsync()</code>).
        </p>
        <p>Consider using <i>placeholders</i> to fix the UI layout while loading.</p>
    </DocAlert>
    <Demo Type="typeof(HxScrollspy_Demo_DynamicContent)" />

    <DocHeading Title="Custom navigation content" />
    <DocAlert>
        When using <code>HxButton</code> inside <code>HxScrollspyNavLink</code>, keep <code>OnClickStopPropagation = "true"</code> (the default).
        If you don't, the <code>HxScrollspyNavLink</code> handles the click as well and scrolls to its anchor after the <code>OnClick</code> handler completes.
    </DocAlert>
    <Demo Type="typeof(HxScrollspy_Demo_CustomNavigationContent)" />
</ApiDoc>

